  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nFalling heights aloft ahead of a deepening, upstream trough and\nwarm, moist conditions within the northwest periphery of a\nBermuda High will once again drive the chances for showers and \nthunderstorms across the region today. While there are still \nsome questions as to the exact coverage due to inconsistent \nmodel guidance, the environment will be very similar to \nyesterday, suggesting the potential for scattered to numerous\nconvective activity. And, given moderate to high instability \n(SBCAPE ~1000 to 2500 J/kg) along with high precipitable water \ncontent (PWATs ~1.5\" to 2+\"), the overall weak forcing should \nnot be a significant inhibiting factor. That being said, this\ncould pose a detriment to the development of severe-level\nconvection. Poor mid-level lapse rates and very weak, deep-layer\nshear, in addition to this weak forcing, will make it difficult\nfor storm organization and strengthening almost everywhere. The\nStorm Prediction Center has, however, introduced a Marginal Risk\n(Level 1 of 5) for Severe Weather across portions of Ulster,\nDutchess, and Litchfield Counties where shear will be greatest.\nHere, an isolated severe storm or two will be possible with\nisolated damaging wind gusts the primary threat. \n\nIn fact, precipitation loading will likely contribute to gusty\nwinds within any shower or storm today regardless of its\nstrength. Likewise, any shower or storm will be capable of\nproducing moderate to locally heavy downpours. The Weather\nPrediction Center has maintained its Marginal Risk (Level 1 of\n4) for Excessive Rainfall across our area for today, suggesting\nthe potential for isolated flash flooding. Given very wet\nantecedent conditions; slow storm motion; and the potential for\ntraining showers and storms, isolated flash flooding is a\nplausible hazard for today particularly for areas that had a\nheavier shower or storm yesterday. \n\nAdditional showers and possible thunderstorms linger into at\nleast the first half of the weekend as the aforementioned trough\nand embedded shortwaves rotate through the region. PWATs won't\nbe to the degree they are today given the Bermuda High looks to\nshift farther to the southeast, but locally moderate downpours\nwill remain possible along with gusty winds. A Marginal Risk for\nExcessive Rainfall remains in place across our region for\nSaturday as well. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe combination of high temperatures widely in the mid to upper\n80s and dewpoints in the 60s to low 70s today will make for\nfairly unpleasant and uncomfortable conditions. However, the \ngreatest feels-like temperatures, or heat index values will be \nfound in portions of the lower Mid-Hudson Valley and southern \nLitchfield County. Here, heat index values in the upper 90s will\nincrease the risk for heat-related illness particularly for \nthose individuals who are susceptible to them. Therefore, a Heat\nAdvisory remains in effect through 8 PM this evening. Drink \nplenty of fluids, limit time outdoors, and take frequent breaks \nin a cooled location in order to mitigate heat stroke and heat \nexhaustion.",
